# Harness ↔ Creator app: capability parity

The Creator app builds its catalog live from `VeniceAPI.fetchCatalog()`, but Venice's
`/models` payload does NOT expose several capabilities. Those come from the harness's
probe-verified registry, which lives at **`~/projects/tools/venice-video-harness/`**
(`src/venice/models.ts` `VIDEO_MODELS` + the `MODELS_SUPPORTING_*` sets in
`src/series/types.ts`).

## How the sync works now (2026-08-06, harness ≥2.15.0)

The harness exports a **capability manifest** — `venice-video capabilities`, or the
committed snapshot `capabilities.json` at its repo root (regenerated by
`npm run manifest`, wired into `prepack`). The app consumes it in three layers
(see `Generation/Catalog/VideoModelCapabilities.swift`):

1. **Manifest, exact ids** — `CapabilityManifestStore` loads the bundled snapshot at
   `Sources/VeniceVideoCreator/Resources/Capabilities/capabilities.json`, and (when the
   user enables "Update model capabilities automatically" in Settings → Models)
   refreshes it at launch from
   `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jordanurbs/venice-video-harness/main/capabilities.json`.
   Schema-gated: a manifest with `schemaVersion` above what the build understands is
   rejected, falling back to the bundled snapshot.
2. **Family-substring fallbacks** in `VideoModelCapabilities` for ids the manifest
   doesn't know — and for the window before the store has loaded, which the catalog
   mapper can run inside.
3. **Conservative default: off.**

## Release checklist

- **Harness registry change** → `npm run manifest` in the harness, commit
  `capabilities.json`, push to main (the raw URL is the app's update channel), and
  copy the snapshot into the app:
  `cp ~/projects/tools/venice-video-harness/capabilities.json ~/projects/products/venice-video-editor/Sources/VeniceVideoCreator/Resources/Capabilities/capabilities.json`
- **Harness DEFAULT / routing change** (a new default video family, longer
  windows, etc. — harness AGENTS.md rule 51) → besides regenerating + copying the
  manifest above, update the app in the SAME change: add/adjust the
  `SupplementalModels` entry for any delisted default model, point
  `ProductionOrchestrator.preferredAutoFamily` at the new family, size
  `MultiShotPlanner.WindowBudget` to it, and refresh the model-selection +
  duration text in `AgentInstructions`. The bundled-snapshot test asserts the
  manifest's `defaults.*` so a copy that forgets these fails CI.
- **Capabilities the manifest does NOT carry into behavior** (deferred request
  builders: `elements[]`, `scene_images`, per-reference audio objects) stay
  hard-off in `VideoModelCapabilities` until the builder lands + a live probe.
- **Breaking manifest SHAPE change** → coordinate a schema-version bump in both repos.
  Additive optional fields may stay schema 1 only with backward-compatibility tests
  and conservative defaults. `supportsCameraTrajectory` is such a field: absent
  means false, and the app enables only the implemented exact Multi-Angle ID.
  Do not publish a sibling-repo schema change without coordinating its release.

## Authority + safety rules

- **Live API truth beats the harness.** If a real Venice response contradicts the
  registry (e.g. a queue 400), fix the harness entry (BOTH `models.ts` and the
  `MODELS_SUPPORTING_*` sets — they can drift from each other, see the Wan 2.7
  end-image case below), regenerate the manifest, update the app's fallbacks, and
  record the probe date + exact error in a comment. The harness is a mirror, not
  the source — it can be wrong.
- **Never enable a capability a model might not have.** Unknown / unlisted ids fall
  through to the conservative default (capability off). Prefer disabling a whole
  uncertain family over risking a wasted paid generation.
- Any capability that changes a paid request body is opt-in behind a flag defaulting
  to current behavior (non-regression is a hard constraint).

## Worked examples

- **2026-07-06 (Wan 2.7 end image):** harness marked the Wan 2.7 i2v family
  `supportsEndImage: true`, but the live queue rejected `end_image_url` ("This model
  does not support end_image_url"). Fix: `supportsEndImage: false` in `models.ts`,
  drop the `wan-2-7` branch from the app allowlist.
- **2026-09-03 (H3 Max resolution ORDER):** capability parity isn't only about which
  values are allowed — the ORDER matters, because `ProductionOrchestrator.reconcile`
  falls back to the first allowed resolution whenever the plan's own value isn't
  offered (which, for tier-label models like MiniMax, is always). Venice reports H3
  Max as `["480P", "768P"]`, so passing live order through defaulted every H3 Max
  shot to its draft tier. Fix: `VideoModelCapabilities.preferredResolutionOrder`
  reorders live to follow the registry's order (manifest `resolutions`, then a family
  fallback for the pre-load window), keeping live-only values but ranking them last.
  Add a preference row whenever a model's live order disagrees with the tier we
  actually want rendered.
- **2026-08-06 (set drift):** the harness's `MODELS_SUPPORTING_END_IMAGE` set STILL
  listed `wan-2-7-image-to-video` even though its `VideoModelSpec` said
  `supportsEndImage: false` — the two harness layers had drifted from each other.
  Caught by the app's bundled-snapshot test
  (`CapabilityManifestTests.bundledSnapshotDecodesAndCoversKeyModels`); fixed in the
  harness set. When registry and sets disagree, trust the probe-dated comment.
